  "answer": "CDK5-dependent NUP98 phosphorylation initiates nuclear-import failure and axonal injury in TARDBP-mutant motor neurons\n\nHypothesis. In human iPSC-derived TARDBP M337V motor neurons, calcium stress activates calpain, converts p35 to p25, and increases CDK5 phosphorylation of pore-incorporated NUP98. This increases passive permeability and slows selective import before cytoplasmic assemblies, ATP loss, or Ran-gradient collapse; nuclear TDP-43 depletion then promotes axonal degeneration and death. The pore modification is the initiating transport lesion; redistribution and injury are downstream. M337V’s ALS relevance, NUP98 barrier biology, and calpain–p35/p25–CDK5 biochemistry are unverified prior knowledge. Their disease-specific linkage, NUP98 substrate identity, and neuronal phosphosites are assumptions.\n\nSupported premises. TDP-43 pathology disrupts nucleoporin localization and transport [S1]. Persistent TDP-43 assemblies can impair import independently of conventional stress granules, accompanying nuclear depletion and death [S3]; stress granules instead sequester transport factors [S4]. Neither establishes initiating nucleoporin phosphorylation; phosphorylated TDP-43 in [S3] is a different modification. C9orf72-associated RanGAP mislocalization supplies a genotype-specific alternative [S2], not evidence for this mechanism.\n\nOrdering and kinase assignment. Compare independently differentiated mutant and isogenic corrected neurons. A bounded NUP98-only discovery stage maps pre-onset, disease-enriched, CDK5-sensitive sites, then freezes exact residues before independent confirmation; another nucleoporin cannot substitute. NPC-enriched, occupancy-calibrated mass spectrometry and scaffold-colocalized phosphosite staining validate a nonperturbing live phosphosite sensor. Track phosphorylation, NUP98 pore residence, NLS-reporter import kinetics, passive permeability, assemblies, nuclear TDP-43, axonal integrity, and survival longitudinally in individual neurons, with matched ATP/Ran measurements. Calcium, calpain activity, p25:p35, and CDK5 activity must precede the mark. Endogenous cleavage-resistant p35 and genetic CDK5 suppression must prevent it; purified active, but not kinase-dead, CDK5–p25 must phosphorylate the same sites.\n\nCausal tests. Use inducible, endogenous-dose phospho-null NUP98 replacement before onset, with sequence-reverted controls; verify abundance, pore incorporation, baseline transport, and removal of mapped phosphorylation while upstream calcium/CDK5 activation remains matched. For sufficiency, acutely recruit active CDK5–p25 to endogenously tagged NUP98 in corrected neurons, reproducing disease-level site occupancy. Actual phosphorylation must promptly reproduce permeability/import defects with wild-type but not phospho-null NUP98; include kinase-dead and no-recruitment controls. Monitor NUP62/NUP153 phosphorylation and localization; emerging compensation requires a baseline-normal factorial block of that change before interpreting failed protection. An independently validated nuclear-retention rescue, restoring functional nuclear TDP-43 to endogenous levels without repairing import, should protect axons and survival, testing mediation.\n\nDiscrimination. Cross NUP98 manipulation with verified stress-granule and persistent-TDP-43-assembly suppression. An intrinsic lesion predicts an early NUP98-dependent defect despite absent assemblies; sequestration predicts assembly-dependent failure [S3,S4]. Compare wild-type and phospho-null nuclei across identical Ran/importin/energy titrations, measuring retained aggregates and transport factors. Factor replenishment alone is not decisive: it may compensate for pore-affinity changes, while retained assemblies may explain persistent defects. C9orf72 neurons provide a context comparator, without requiring shared dependence [S2].\n\nFalsification. Define onset as two consecutive import-rate measurements below a pilot-derived corrected-control threshold; preregister biologically meaningful equivalence margins and power confirmation using pilot variance. Verified pre-onset phosphoblockade leaving import failure equivalent to mutant controls, with compensation excluded, falsifies initiation. Verified disease-matched phosphorylation without the early transport phenotype falsifies sufficiency. Normal p25/CDK5 activity despite detectable disease phosphorylation, with sensitive positive controls, rejects the stipulated kinase-activation route. Failure of verified functional nuclear-TDP-43 restoration to protect neurons rejects depletion as the proposed injury mediator.\n\nLimitations. Negative phosphosite detection only lowers confidence: brief or low-occupancy marks may escape calibrated assays. Sensor perturbation, conditional replacement, compensation, and kinase recruitment require validation. Phosphorylation may be downstream of broader stress; human chronology cannot be inferred from iPSC timing or postmortem tissue. This restricted preclinical hypothesis establishes neither a universal ALS cause nor a treatment.",
